</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#7-fit-a-proper-loft-ladder">7. Fit a Proper Loft Ladder</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#8-protect-what-you-store-from-heat-and-damp">8. Protect What You Store From Heat and Damp</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#loft-conversion-and-eaves-storage-ideas">Loft Conversion and Eaves Storage Ideas</a><ul class="kb-table-of-contents-list-sub"><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#9-build-drawers-into-the-eaves">9. Build Drawers Into the Eaves</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#10-line-eaves-cupboards-with-boxes">10. Line Eaves Cupboards With Boxes</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#11-fit-shelves-that-follow-the-slope">11. Fit Shelves That Follow the Slope</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#12-choose-a-bed-with-storage">12. Choose a Bed With Storage</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#13-put-wardrobes-where-the-ceiling-is-highest">13. Put Wardrobes Where the Ceiling Is Highest</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#small-loft-and-rented-home-ideas">Small Loft and Rented Home Ideas</a><ul class="kb-table-of-contents-list-sub"><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#14-board-just-the-area-by-the-hatch">14. Board Just the Area by the Hatch</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#15-use-vacuum-bags-for-bulky-soft-items">15. Use Vacuum Bags for Bulky Soft Items</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#16-do-a-yearly-loft-check">16. Do a Yearly Loft Check</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#common-loft-mistakes">Common Loft Mistakes</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#loft-faq">Loft FAQ</a><ul class="kb-table-of-contents-list-sub"><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#can-you-put-boards-on-top-of-loft-insulation">Can you put boards on top of loft insulation?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#do-you-need-building-regulations-to-board-a-loft">Do you need building regulations to board a loft?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#what-should-you-not-store-in-a-loft">What should you not store in a loft?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#how-do-you-organise-a-small-loft">How do you organise a small loft?</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#final-thoughts">Final Thoughts</a></li></ul></div></nav>


<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Board a Loft for Storage Safely</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Before you add a single box, check how the loft is built. Most loft floors are just ceiling joists with insulation between them, designed to hold up the ceiling below, not a floor full of storage.</p>



<figure class="dsh-diagram"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 1200 900" font-family="Poppins, sans-serif" role="img" aria-labelledby="lf-t lf-d"><title id="lf-t">How to board a loft for storage</title><desc id="lf-d">Cross-section of a loft boarded for storage. Ceiling joists sit on the ceiling below. Insulation fills between the joists and a cross layer goes on top, about 270 mm in total for mineral wool. Loft legs raise the storage boards above the insulation so it is not squashed. A gap is left at the eaves so air can flow and stop condensation. Light storage boxes go on the boards; keep loads light, keep clear of flues, and check with building control before converting the loft into a room.</desc><rect width="1200" height="900" fill="#F7F1E9"/><text x="60" y="88" font-size="44" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">Board a Loft for Storage</text><text x="60" y="128" font-size="22" fill="#6E645C">Raise the boards so the insulation stays full depth</text><text x="1140" y="60" font-size="18" fill="#6E645C" text-anchor="end">dailyspacehacks.com</text><path d="M70 620 L600 170 L1130 620" fill="none" stroke="#B98A5E" stroke-width="14" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M90 620 L600 188 L1110 620 Z" fill="#FBF7F0"/><rect x="60" y="640" width="1080" height="14" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="2"/><text x="60" y="684" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">Ceiling of the room below</text><rect x="215" y="532" width="770" height="108" fill="#F2D27A" opacity="0.8"/><rect x="150" y="600" width="16" height="40" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="262" y="600" width="16" height="40" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="374" y="600" width="16" height="40" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="486" y="600" width="16" height="40" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="598" y="600" width="16" height="40" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="710" y="600" width="16" height="40" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="822" y="600" width="16" height="40" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="934" y="600" width="16" height="40" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="10