<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Organise a Loft in 6 Steps</h2>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Clear a landing space.</strong> Bring everything down to a spare room or landing so you can see what you have.</li>


<li><strong>Declutter.</strong> Donate, sell or recycle what nobody has used in the last few years.</li>


<li><strong>Check the floor.</strong> Make sure you have a safe walkway of boards on the joists, raised above the insulation.</li>


<li><strong>Box it up.</strong> Use identical clear lidded boxes, one category per box, and label the ends.</li>


<li><strong>Zone by use.</strong> Things you need every year go nearest the hatch; rarely used items go furthest away.</li>


<li><strong>Keep a list.</strong> Note what is in each box on your phone so you only go up when you need to.</li>
</ol>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1536" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-06-before-after.jpg" alt="Loft before and after: bin bags and cardboard boxes on flattened insulation on the left, the same loft with a raised boarded walkway and clear boxes on raised boards on the right" class="wp-image-2523" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-06-before-after.jpg 1536w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-06-before-after-300x200.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-06-before-after-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-06-before-after-768x512.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1536px) 100vw, 1536px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If the job feels too big, start with our guide on <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/how-to-declutter-your-home/">how to declutter your home</a> and tackle the loft last, once you know what is worth keeping.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Loft Storage Ideas for Boarded Lofts</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">1. Raise the Boards on Loft Legs</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Loft legs are plastic or metal stilts that screw onto the joists and hold the boards above the insulation, so you get a storage floor without squashing it. They are a popular way to board a loft that already has a full 270 mm of insulation. Keep the boarded area to a central walkway and leave the edges near the eaves clear.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">2. Use Identical Clear Lidded Boxes</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Matching clear boxes stack neatly, keep out dust and let you see what is inside with a torch. Lids also help keep out insects. Choose a size you can carry up a loft ladder with one hand free, and avoid open cardboard boxes, which soften in damp air.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">3. Add Low Shelving Between the Rafters</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-03-low-shelving.jpg" alt="Fully boarded loft with a low pine shelving unit of clear boxes and wicker baskets tucked under the sloping rafters" class="wp-image-2520" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-03-low-shelving.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-03-low-shelving-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-03-low-shelving-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-03-low-shelving-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A low, freestanding shelving unit on the boards doubles your storage without stacking boxes four high. Keep it below the slope of the roof, put the heaviest boxes on the bottom shelf and leave a gap behind it so air can move. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/diy-closet-shelves/">DIY shelves guide</a> shows simple builds you can adapt.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">4. Zone the Loft by How Often You Need Things</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Put the things you fetch every year, such as Christmas decorations and suitcases, closest to the hatch. Keep rarely used items like keepsakes and spare bedding further back. For the festive boxes, our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/christmas-decoration-storage/">Christmas decoration storage</a> ideas help you pack them well.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">5. Keep a Clear Central Walkway</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A straight path of boards from the hatch to the far end means you never have to step between joists, where a foot can go straight through the plasterboard ceiling below. Store boxes either side of the walkway, never on it.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">6. Light It Properly</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Poor light makes slips more likely. A battery LED lantern or a rechargeable light is the simple fix; a fixed light switched from the landing should be fitted by a qualified electrician. North Tyneside Council does not allow sockets in tenants&#8217; boarded lofts. Any new wiring should be done by a qualified electrician, and make sure boards and insulation never cover existing cables or junction boxes.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">7. Fit a Proper Loft Ladder</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Carrying boxes up a stepladder is the riskiest part of using a loft. A folding loft ladder with a handrail makes access safer and quicker. If you need a new hatch, check current building regulations first, as the council guidance points out.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">8. Protect What You Store From Heat and Damp</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Lofts get hot in summer and cold and damp in winter. The Library of Congress <a href="https://www.loc.gov/preservation/care/paper.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">advises against storing paper in attics and basements</a>, so keep photos, documents and keepsakes in the house. Store bedding and soft toys in sealed bags inside boxes, and keep candles, paint and electronics out of the loft altogether.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Loft Conversion and Eaves Storage Ideas</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A converted loft has the same awkward slopes but more chances for built-in storage. These ideas use the low spaces under the roof that are too short to stand in.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">9. Build Drawers Into the Eaves</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-02-eaves-drawers.jpg" alt="Loft conversion bedroom with a row of white built-in drawers set into the low eaves wall beneath a roof window" class="wp-image-2519" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-02-eaves-drawers.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-02-eaves-drawers-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-02-eaves-drawers-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-02-eaves-drawers-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Pull-out drawers set into the low knee wall turn dead space under the slope into a chest of drawers that takes up no floor space. They suit loft bedrooms where a wardrobe will not fit. See our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/box-room-bedroom-ideas/">box room bedroom ideas</a> for other ways to make a small bedroom work.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">10. Line Eaves Cupboards With Boxes</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-04-eaves-cupboard.jpg" alt="Inside an eaves cupboard with baskets and clear boxes of folded blankets lined up on a plywood floor, getting smaller where the slope gets low" class="wp-image-2521" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-04-eaves-cupboard.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-04-eaves-cupboard-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-04-eaves-cupboard-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-04-eaves-cupboard-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Behind a small door, the triangular eaves space is ideal for suitcases and seasonal items. Put the tallest boxes at the front and the smallest at the back where the roof gets low, and add a battery puck light so you can see in.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">11. Fit Shelves That Follow the Slope</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-05-sloped-shelves.jpg" alt="Loft landing with white built-in shelves stepping down under a sloping ceiling, holding books, baskets and plants beside a roof window" class="wp-image-2522" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-05-sloped-shelves.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-05-sloped-shelves-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-05-sloped-shelves-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/loft-storage-ideas-05-sloped-shelves-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Built-in shelves that step down with the roof line use every centimetre of a sloping wall on a loft landing or in a bedroom. Keep heavy books on the lower shelves and display pieces at the top.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">12. Choose a Bed With Storage</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In a loft bedroom, an ottoman or drawer bed holds bedding and out-of-season clothes without using wall space you do not have. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-bedroom-storage-ideas/">small bedroom storage ideas</a> cover the best styles.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">13. Put Wardrobes Where the Ceiling Is Highest</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Long coats need roughly 1.5 to 1.7 m of hanging height, so measure first and place a wardrobe or open rail under the ridge and use the low sides for drawers and baskets. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wardrobe-depth/">wardrobe depth calculator</a> checks how deep it needs to be.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Small Loft and Rented Home Ideas</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">14. Board Just the Area by the Hatch</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you rent or your loft is small, you do not need to board all of it. A few raised boards around the hatch give you space for lightweight boxes; ask your landlord before fixing anything. For more space-saving ideas inside the home, try our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/under-stairs-storage-ideas/">under stairs storage ideas</a> and <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/airing-cupboard-shelves/">airing cupboard shelves</a>.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">15. Use Vacuum Bags for Bulky Soft Items</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Duvets, pillows and winter coats take up most of a small loft. Vacuum bags shrink them to a fraction of the size (avoid long-term compression for down and wool); put the bags inside a lidded box so they do not get punctured.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">16. Do a Yearly Loft Check</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Once a year, ideally when you bring the Christmas boxes down, check for damp patches, droppings or damage and pass on anything you no longer need. If you are moving soon, our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/moving-box-calculator/">moving box calculator</a> helps you plan the boxes.</p>



