<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common Loft Mistakes</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Laying boards straight on top of insulation.</strong> Squashed insulation keeps your home less warm.</li>


<li><strong>Overloading the joists.</strong> Ceiling joists are not a floor; keep storage light.</li>


<li><strong>Blocking the eaves.</strong> Boxes against the eaves can stop air flowing and lead to condensation.</li>


<li><strong>Storing paper and photos up there.</strong> Heat and damp damage them; keep them in the house.</li>


<li><strong>Using open cardboard boxes.</strong> They soften, collapse and let in insects.</li>


<li><strong>Stepping between the joists.</strong> Stay on the boards so you do not go through the ceiling.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Loft FAQ</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Can you put boards on top of loft insulation?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You should not lay boards directly on top of loft insulation, because squashing it reduces how well it insulates. Instead, fit loft legs or a raised floor on the joists so the boards sit above the insulation, which Which? recommends at 270 mm for mineral wool, and leave the eaves clear for ventilation.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Do you need building regulations to board a loft?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Planning Portal says loft joists are usually not designed for significant extra weight, so boarding over them for storage may need building regulations approval. Converting a loft into a room always needs approval, so check with building control before you start.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What should you not store in a loft?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Avoid storing photos, documents, paper keepsakes, candles, paint, aerosols, electronics and anything heavy in the loft. Heat, cold and damp damage paper and electronics, and heavy items can overload the ceiling joists. Keep the loft for light, robust items such as suitcases, bedding in sealed bags and seasonal decorations.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How do you organise a small loft?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Board a walkway by the hatch on raised loft legs, use identical clear lidded boxes and keep a list of what is in each box. Store the items you need every year nearest the hatch and rarely used things further back, and use vacuum bags to shrink bulky bedding.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The best loft storage ideas start with a safe floor: raised boards, full-depth insulation and clear eaves. After that, matching boxes, simple zones and a yearly check keep the loft useful instead of a place where things disappear.</p>



